 the video.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.800
Before I start my question, 
let me just briefly

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.620
introduce myself so that 
 that you can have a

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.860
general idea on my background.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.860
I'm a senior to be 
undergraduate student from

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.800
Shanghai Tech University,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.980
and is now studying at 
Harvard as a visiting

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.240
undergraduate. I'm majoring
in biology, while I'm also

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.220
a big fan of entrepreneurship
and management.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.500
Because personally, I consider
entrepreneurship and science

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.240
as something closely related.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.000
Say, for scientists,
actually they need to think

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.320
from an entrepreneur point of view

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.320
so that your discoveries can be
 made into something useful.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.020
While for the entrepreneurs,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.610
sometimes they do need 
advanced scientific tools to

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.620
make their normal ideas into reality.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.615
The good thing is that from 
the past decades,

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.840
we do see a lot of good examples

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.440
where people combine entrepreneurship 
and science together,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.320
typically in the IT field.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:25.120
However, the interesting phenomenon
 is that in the field of biology,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.920
this philosophy doesn't 
work well for some reason.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:36.540
My question is just,  "What do you 
think might  be the main factors

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.640
that caused these phenomena
 in the bio world?"

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.540
And also, from your point of view,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.620
"What do you consider as the biggest, 
also the most promising

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.460
opportunities in the field of biology

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.580
in the following two or three decades?"

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.960
Looking forward to your reply and many thanks.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.480
- [Gary] Haolong, thank you so much 
for the question.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:02.900
This is going to be a fun episode.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.760
I have a feeling everybody's going to 
enjoy these video questions.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.920
Look, I think the reason that you're 
seeing a lot of people in the biology sector,

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.120
...biotech,  things of that nature...

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.320
struggle is at people hold onto romance.  
People hold on to the establishment.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:22.180
The decision makers in your field
often times don't want to change.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.620
In entrepreneurship --
that energy is always disruptive,

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:30.580
and often times takes people that 
are on a pedestal, off that pedestal.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.520
There's human behaviors of defense.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.440
You have to understand that once 
somebody is at a pedestal that they feel is

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.280
the height of where they are,  
they start going on defense

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.920
and not letting anybody
take them off.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.580
They're not continuing to aspire.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.600
I believe a lot of it 
is human inefficiency.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.160
As far as open opportunities,
the truth is, I believe that

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.380
in the biology, what you see
because of technology and the

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.500
internet infrastructure --- the 
enormous advances we have

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.540
in science is,
 everything is vulnerable.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.360
I often say in entrepreneurship, 
which I understand much better than biology,

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.320
that Amazon put bookstores
 on notice early.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:11.840
Uber put car service, black limo 
companies, cab services on notice.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.480
But everybody is vulnerable. 
I would argue because of the

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:20.560
advances in technology, literally
everything in every sector is

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.220
today vulnerable over the next 
two decades.  And so for me,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.080
I would take your energy 
and deploy it against the thing

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:28.400
you're most passionate about.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.660
Because it inevitably will be disrupted 
over the next two to three decades.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.300
- [Yang] Hey, Gary! 
This is Yang from Shanghai.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.680
I have a question.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.480
For AI startup, it's very difficult 
for them to collect a lot

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.040
a lot of data in their 
first 3 or 5 years, and

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.940
they really need these data to 
improve their products in the algorithm.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.480
Big companies like Google-

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.560
they already have a lot of data 
and they can develop similar

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.400
products using this data easily.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.860
"What's your advice for these
 AI startups?" Thank you!

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.100
- [Gary] Chen Yang, my advice is to
 not fight Google in a game

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.360
that Google is more likely to win. 
The number one rule I have about

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:13.100
startups and entrepreneurs is 
when David is fighting Goliath,

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.120
you never fight Goliath's game.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.360
Your question, if anybody was 
paying attention to, makes this very easy,

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.140
which is: Creating a business 
that is vulnerable to the

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.700
Google infrastructure or any other 
company for that matter,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.220
whether it's Amazon or Apple or Facebook.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.740
It's just not smart, and so what 
you need to do, is you find small niches

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.700
that either you hope you can
move so quickly in, that by the

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.980
time they decide to attack it,
they're better to acquire you.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.960
Or that you've amassed so much
market share that you'll be able to

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.580
sustain the blow of that competition, 
and that whatever you're left with,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.780
once they enter, will still be meaningful.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.180
Very similar to the case study.
 I'll give you a great one.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.260
The case study is Compaq Computers
 versus IBM in the 80's.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:58.840
They found their niches. 
They found their product fit,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.500
and they were able to go up 
against "The Big Blue Giant".

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.980
Then they also used software
companies like Microsoft

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.720
to really democratize and create a 
huge opportunity and they teamed up

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.040
with their competition. I think if you 
want to spend time on Google

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.380
or whatever search engine 
you'd like,  to look at the

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.660
Compaq Computer story of how
 they were able to navigate and

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:22.040
grow to such a ... That is a perfect story
and we see it everyday.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.460
It's speed. Only speed

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:28.750
trumps somebody that's bigger
 than a giant. Play your game,

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:33.640
and so find niches and go extremely 
fast. Otherwise you have no shot.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.580
-[DRock] (Inaudible) ...Compaq.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.740
-[Gary] Good story. 
Yeah, you should really look into it.

